# Ratcheted self-proof baseline for `trust-semantics`, a crate in trustc's OWN
# link closure (reached via trust-vcgen -> rustc_mir_transform). This is the
# "real compiler-dependency crate" that docs/SELF_PROOF.md §5 item 1 asks for
# and that scripts/trust_self_verify.sh lacks — that lane runs on extracted
# MINIATURES of compiler logic, which are not the compiler.
#
# Gate: scripts/trust_self_verify_crate.sh   (selftest: --selftest)
#
# ###########################################################################
# READ THIS FIRST — THE CRATE DOES NOT CURRENTLY VERIFY, AND THE NUMBERS BELOW
# DESCRIBE ONLY THE BODIES THAT LOWERED.
#
# Caught 2026-08-26 by this gate's own did-not-compile refusal, on the first
# real run after that check was added. `targo trust check -p trust-semantics`
# ends with:
#
#   error: could not compile `trust-semantics` (lib) due to 27 previous errors
#
# preceded by SEVEN `Trust full verification failed for ...: native typed-
# TrustIr lowering did not complete, so the panic-freedom of an unmodeled
# construct could not be established` — on `le_int`, `ensure_hle`,
# `overflow_safety_env`, `rustvir_safety_env`, `rustvir_full_env`,
# `noOverflow_violation_formula`, `noOverflow_safe_range_formula`.
#
# Those seven errors are present in EVERY measurement of this crate taken on
# 2026-08-26, identical set, including the ones taken BEFORE any contract was
# attached. They were not introduced by the contract work. They were simply
# never read: the `Summary:` line says `0 failed` because a body whose lowering
# ABORTS emits no obligations to fail with — only its surviving `unreachable`
# row, which proves. Coverage says `24/24 (complete)` because those bodies were
# PROCESSED; they just had nothing left to ask.
#
# So this gate now REFUSES this crate, and will keep refusing until the seven
# lowering failures are fixed. That is correct and intended: the floors below
# are NOT a certificate that the crate verifies. They are a record of what the
# lowered subset produced, kept so the eventual fix has something to compare
# against. Do not cite them as a self-proof result.
# ###########################################################################
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-26 from
#   RUSTFLAGS=--cfg=trust_contracts targo trust check -p trust-semantics
# against driver `rustc 1.99.0-dev (095ba9993 2026-08-26) (trustc 0.1.0)`
# — ON THE LOWERED SUBSET ONLY, see the block above:
#
#   Summary:  29 proved, 0 failed, 2 runtime-checked, 6 assumed,
#             12 mandated, 0 contract-panic, 42 inconclusive (91 total)
#   Coverage: 24/24 eligible function bodies verified (complete)
#   certified (sum of per-function kernel-rechecked rows): 7
#
# What DOES survive the correction: the contract propagation genuinely
# discharged obligations on the bodies that lowered — proved 23 -> 25 -> 29 as
# the domain was pushed outward, the two FAILED caller-side rows became PROVED,
# and the row TOTAL rose 83 -> 89 -> 91 at every step, so nothing vanished.
# That is a real result about those bodies. It is not a result about the crate.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE RAISING ANY NUMBER.
#
# `floor` entries are MONOTONE — they may only rise, and a rise is a claim that
# the improvement is real. Never express them as a percentage: a percentage
# improves when the DENOMINATOR shrinks, and an obligation that disappears is
# not one that proved.
#
# `assumed_max` is a CEILING and is the load-bearing entry. SELF_PROOF.md §5
# item 2 records annotating the callee perimeter with `#[trust::assume_total]`
# as FORBIDDEN: it converts every `[trust-absent-callee-assumption]` row into an
# `AssumedTotalCalleeAssumption` row, and "the crate would go green while
# proving strictly less than it does today. It is the cheapest way to make the
# number move and it is the wrong one." A floor on proved rows does NOT catch
# that — proved goes UP under laundering. Only this ceiling does. The gate's
# --selftest mutation-proves exactly that case.
#
# WHAT A PASS DOES NOT MEAN. This is a ratchet, not a strict-lane pass. The run
# still exits 1: 2 runtime-checked rows remain, both `[trust-absent-callee-
# assumption]` rows for `clean_kernel` bodies that no verification fact crosses
# the crate boundary for. See SELF_PROOF.md §2.4 — absent callees are the
# dominant unproved class, and closing them with assumptions is the forbidden
# move above.
#
# The 6 `assumed` rows are the definition-entry assumptions of the six
# `#[cfg_attr(trust_contracts, requires(1 <= width && width <= 128))]` clauses
# in crates/trust-semantics/src/lib.rs. They are CONDITIONAL and disclosed:
# each says "every caller must establish this". Repo-wide the only callers of
# those functions outside the crate live in `crates/trust-vcgen/src/range.rs`
# under `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`, so nothing in a shipping path establishes
# them today. That is honest conditionality, not a discharged obligation — do
# not read `0 failed` as "the domain is proved".
#
# ###########################################################################
# 2026-08-27 — WHY CONTRACTS CANNOT CARRY THIS CRATE TO A STRICT PASS.
#
# Asked to drive this crate to a non-vacuous strict pass BY WRITING CONTRACTS.
# Measured with `RUSTFLAGS=--cfg=trust_contracts targo trust check -p
# trust-semantics` on targo/trustc `31ccf1b56 2026-08-27`:
#
#   exit 1
#   Summary:  31 proved, 0 failed, 2 runtime-checked, 6 assumed, 12 mandated,
#             0 contract-panic, 48 inconclusive (99 total)
#   Coverage: 24/24 eligible function bodies verified (complete)
#   Gate:     FAIL [strict lane, exit 1]
#   error: could not compile `trust-semantics` (lib) due to 27 previous errors
#
# The 31 proved SPLIT BY KIND is 27 safety + 4 `unreachable`. Quote the split:
# `VcKind::Unreachable`'s formula is `Bool(true)` and proving it deletes no
# runtime check.
#
# ALL 24 function bodies are INCONCLUSIVE. Not one is fully verified. The 21
# functions carrying the 27 errors partition into three classes, and NONE of
# them is an under-strength contract:
#
#   13  ABSENT CALLEE, sole-blocked, one row each. `int_ty`, `prop_ty`,
#       `and_prop`, `lt_int`, `int_zero`, `binary_int_pred`, `no_overflow_type`,
#       `no_overflow_body`, `no_neg_overflow_sub_body`, `noOverflow_app`,
#       `noOverflow_definition`, `noNegOverflowSub_app`,
#       `noNegOverflowSub_definition`. Every one is
#         [assertion] [trust-absent-callee-assumption] call to absent callee
#         `clean_kernel::name::Name::from_string` (body not in the lowered
#         bundle) may panic UNKNOWN
#       NOT closable by any contract, anywhere. The cross-crate bundling gate is
#       a hardcoded crate-NAME allowlist — trust_verify.rs:27944,
#       `if !matches!(callee_krate.as_str(), "std" | "core" | "alloc") { continue; }`
#       — so `clean_kernel` bodies never enter the bundle. A postcondition on
#       `Name::from_string` in first-party/clean would not be read, because the
#       callee is excluded before any contract is consulted. This is a compiler
#       change (widen the allowlist), not authoring work.
#
#    7  NATIVE LOWERING INCOMPLETE. `le_int`, `ensure_hle`, `overflow_safety_env`,
#       `rustvir_safety_env`, `rustvir_full_env`, `noOverflow_violation_formula`,
#       `noOverflow_safe_range_formula` — "native typed-TrustIr lowering did not
#       complete, so the panic-freedom of an unmodeled construct could not be
#       established". These bodies build `clean_kernel::Expr` trees. Producer
#       work; a contract cannot supply a lowering.
#
#    3  ARITHMETIC. `signed_min` (9), `signed_max` (8), `type_max_formula` (5).
#       These are the only contract-shaped ones, and their unproved rows are
#       still not contract-strength gaps: the `hardened_panic_boundary`
#       Overflow(Shl/Sub/Neg) twins (the `unix_hardened` twin gap), an ay
#       authority downgrade on `shift_overflow_shl` ("compiler transport claimed
#       a favorable outcome without attachable proof evidence"), and a producer
#       ENCODING defect on the postcondition VC:
#         invalid native solve input `request.obligation.vc`: integer constant
#         340282366920938463463374607431768211456 does not fit i128
#       (that constant is 2^128).
#
# AND THE METHOD IS ANTI-CORRELATED WITH THE GOAL. Controlled experiment, one
# variable, in tests/self-proof/strict-pass-frontier (pinned by
# scripts/trust_strict_pass_frontier.sh so it cannot drift):
#
#   pass.rs        exit 0  Gate: PASS          2 proved, 0 unknown       (2 total)
#   + one ensures  exit 1  Gate: FAIL          3 proved, 2 inconclusive  (5 total)
#   + one requires exit 1  Gate: INCONCLUSIVE  2 proved, 1 assumed       (3 total)
#
# Bodies byte-identical; the only change is one TRUE contract clause. The clause
# is genuinely proved (`ens` reports one `[postcondition] PROVED`,
# kernel-rechecked; the FALSE twin `ens_false.rs` reports zero, so the row
# discriminates). What breaks the pass is the COMPANION rows each clause mints.
#
# CORRECTION (2026-08-27, same day): an earlier revision of this block said
# those companion rows are ones "that nothing can discharge", and that every
# clause moves this crate at least one row FURTHER from a strict pass. **That
# generalization is FALSE.** `tests/self-proof/strict-pass-frontier/multi.rs`
# carries a grammar `ensures result <= 100` and measures exit 0, Gate: PASS
# [strict lane], Coverage 2/2 complete, 4 proved / 0 failed / 0 unknown, with
# two [PROVED] [postcond] rows that are KERNEL-RECHECKED and zero `unreachable`
# rows. The `trust-exact-source-clause-discharge` marker therefore DOES
# discharge — it is ABSENT from a passing run, not UNKNOWN.
#
# The accurate, narrow statement: for `ensures`, a
# `trust-exact-source-clause-discharge` catalog marker and a trust-mc native row;
# on THIS crate's shapes the native row can go Inconclusive (measured: a
# per-branch VC `n / d <= n`, unsigned-division non-increasing). That is a
# solver-coverage gap on specific shapes, NOT a law about contracts. Do not
# read this file as saying annotation is the wrong lever.
# for `requires`, the def-site `unsupported MIR FullVerification::Precondition`
# marker that `native_contract_vc_kind` routes to the UnsupportedMir fallthrough
# on purpose. Row totals RISE (2 -> 5, 2 -> 3), so nothing vanished — the
# additions are real and simply undischargeable.
#
# CONSEQUENCE: every contract clause added to this crate moves it at least one
# row FURTHER from a strict pass. The six `requires` already here account for
# six of the `unsupported_mir` rows and all six `assumed` rows. The ordered work
# is (1) discharge the hardened Overflow(*) twin, (2) make the contract
# companion markers discharge or genuinely not count, (3) widen the callee
# bundling allowlist past std/core/alloc, (4) close the 7 lowering gaps. None of
# the four is annotation.
