# EXPERIMENT A — `vec![..]` -> `Vec::from([..])`.  REJECTED, REVERTED.
#
# CAUSE (3) "TRUSTIR HAS NO UNION REPRESENTATION" AND CAUSE (4) "TWO MIR ASSERT
# KINDS NOT MODELLED BY VCGEN" ARE ONE SOURCE-LEVEL IDIOM, NOT TWO COMPILER
# GAPS. Both are wholly attributable to the NON-EMPTY arm of `vec![..]`. This
# crate had exactly five such sites, in four functions — `le_int` (1),
# `ensure_hle` (2), `noOverflow_violation_formula` (1),
# `noOverflow_safe_range_formula` (1) — which is precisely the function set
# causes (3) and (4) name. `library/alloc/src/macros.rs:59` expands
# `vec![a, b]` to
#   box_assume_init_into_vec_unsafe(write_box_via_move(Box::new_uninit(), [a,b]))
# and `write_box_via_move` is a `#[rustc_intrinsic]` that rustc_mir_build lowers
# INTO THE CALLER'S OWN MIR (`expr/into.rs`, `sym::write_box_via_move`) as
#   (*b).value.value.value = val;
# — a `Box<MaybeUninit<[T; N]>>` Deref plus union-field projections, appearing in
# a `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` crate that declares no union and boxes nothing.
# Empty `vec![]` is a different macro arm (`Vec::new()`) and is harmless.
#
# `Vec::from([..])` is `<[T]>::into_vec(Box::new(s))`: same `Vec`, but the
# allocation and its obligations sit in `Vec::from`'s own body instead of inline
# in ours. Meaning preservation is externally pinned — all 7 crate tests pass,
# including `test_violation_formula_matches_range_rs_shape`, which compares the
# `Vec::from`-built production term against a `vec![..]`-built expectation.
#
# MEASURED:
#   exit 1                                                   (unchanged)
#   errors                            28 -> 24
#   Coverage                          24/24 complete         (unchanged)
#   Summary  20 proved, 0 failed, 1 runtime-checked, 6 assumed, 12 mandated,
#            0 contract-panic, 32 inconclusive (71 total)
#   ROW TOTAL                        104 -> 71   (35 LOST, 2 GAINED, 0 CHANGED)
#   PROVED                            33 -> 20;  real safety 29 -> 16
#   `field projection on union MaybeUninit`   26 -> 0
#   `UnmodeledSafetyAssert(...)`              20 -> 0
#   `le_int` and `ensure_hle` LOWER for the first time — both lost their
#     "native typed-TrustIr lowering did not complete" error and each GAINED the
#     honest absent-callee row it previously could not emit.
#
# THE ERROR COUNT IMPROVED AND THE CRATE GOT WORSE. 27 of the 35 lost rows are
# the vec!-desugar's own memory obligations at alloc/src/macros.rs:59; that
# operation is gone, so arguably so should they be. THE OTHER EIGHT ARE NOT:
#
#   4  [PROVED] [hardened_unsafe_operation] (clean-kernel-certified)
#      [unsafe:sep:alloc] allocation result null check for
#      `alloc::boxed::Box::<Formula>::new` in `noOverflow_violation_formula`
#   4  the same, in `noOverflow_safe_range_formula`
#
# Each of those two functions contains exactly four `Box::new` calls and had
# exactly four such rows. THOSE CALL SITES WERE NOT TOUCHED BY THE PATCH. The
# allocations still happen; their kernel-certified null-check proofs simply stop
# being emitted (`unsafe:sep:alloc` occurrences 16 -> 0). Un-emitted is
# unchecked — and it is invisible in the one number that moved the right way.
# A reader optimizing `28 -> 24` ships this.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXPERIMENT B — the 12 `mandated` rows, `.ok()` -> `?`.  REJECTED, REVERTED.
#
# The mandate fires on a LITERAL NEEDLE, `"core::result::Result::ok"`
# (crates/trust-vcgen/src/hardened.rs `CallRule`). So ANY rewrite that removes
# the `.ok()` call removes the row — including a `match` that still drops the
# error. That is needle evasion, not discharge. Nor can the row be discharged in
# place: this run's own evidence note for
# `vc:trust_semantics__rustvir_safety_env:error_discard:1` reads "TrustSpecPredicate
# lowered to boolean true; no trust-mc CHC error condition was emitted" — there is
# no condition to prove. The only non-evasive rewrite is real PROPAGATION, i.e.
# three `pub fn`s changing from `Option<Environment>` to
# `Result<Environment, EnvError>`.
#
# Measured that anyway (all 7 tests still pass):
#   exit 1                                                   (unchanged)
#   errors                            28 -> 28               (UNCHANGED)
#   Coverage                          24/24 complete         (unchanged)
#   Summary  33 proved, 0 failed, 1 runtime-checked, 6 assumed, 0 mandated,
#            0 contract-panic, 52 inconclusive (92 total)
#   ROW TOTAL                        104 -> 92   (-12, exactly the 12 mandate
#            rows; the 4 other lost/gained keys are BlockId renumbering twins,
#            17->16 / 24->23 / 64->62 / 65->63, same kind and same outcome)
#   PROVED                            33 -> 33   (UNCHANGED, 29 real + 4 unreach)
#
# Twelve rows deleted, ZERO errors removed, zero obligations discharged. The
# `mandated` bucket contributes to the per-function "N obligation(s) were not
# fully verified" counts (`overflow_safety_env` 5->2, `rustvir_full_env` 8->2,
# `rustvir_safety_env` 3->2) but never to the ERROR COUNT, so removing it buys
# nothing and costs the only record that the hazard is there.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS LEAVES. Causes (1) absent-callee, (2) the bundle-admission refusal
# "member has a guarded or non-entry-prefix Assert" (crates/trust-ir-bridge/src/
# lower.rs) that poisons `type_min_formula`/`type_max_formula` through
# `signed_min`/`signed_max`, and (5) the ay/trust-mc shift encoding are untouched
# by either experiment and remain compiler/solver-side. Cause (2) is not
# authoring-reachable either: making `signed_max`'s Assert unguarded and
# entry-prefix means deleting the branch structure that makes the body TOTAL,
# reintroducing the panics the totality rewrite removed — and that admission path
# only derives `Not(Eq(v, 0))` divisor shapes, so an unguarded `Shl` Assert has no
# admitted shape there in the first place.
#
# NET: source-level authoring CAN move this crate's error count (28 -> 24, ~14%),
# and the mechanism is now named and located. It cannot do so without deleting
# obligations, which is exactly what the sentence above claimed. Both patches were
# reverted; neither is in the tree. Do not re-derive Experiment A from the error
# count alone.
# ###########################################################################

# NOTE 2026-09-29 (NOT a re-mint; the numbers below are unchanged). Trust `main`
# stopped minting the `[unsafe:sep:alloc]` null row for `Box::new` (docs/TCB.md,
# "Allocation failure is an abort, not a panic"): that row was the allocator
# premise conjoined onto its own negation, a tautology, and the 8 of them this
# crate carried (the EXPERIMENT A block above: four `Box::new` each in
# `noOverflow_violation_formula` / `noOverflow_safe_range_formula`) are counted in
# `proved_min`. A toolchain built from that commit reads `proved` lower by exactly
# those rows (and `certified` lower by any of them that were kernel-rechecked).
# That is NOT the "un-emitted is unchecked" regression EXPERIMENT A warns about —
# the allocation's non-null result is still used by every later deref of the box,
# it simply is not re-minted as a demand whose proof checks nothing — so re-mint
# the floor at that promotion, citing this note; do not restore the rows. Not
# re-measured here: this gate needs a verifying toolchain built from that commit.
certified_min = 7
proved_min    = 29
assumed_max   = 6
